    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0277",
      "sequence": 289,
      "meaning": "Rāma asks the sage to show affection to the boy and not be angry with one so guileless and milk-faced. Had the child known anything of the sage’s power, would he have dared treat him as an equal? When children make some mischief, teacher, father and mother are delighted at heart. Therefore show grace, recognizing him as a child and servant; who is as virtuous, steadfast and wise as you? Rāma’s words cool Paraśurāma somewhat, but Lakṣmaṇa says something and smiles again. Seeing the smile, wrath fills the sage from toenail to crown: ‘Rāma, your brother is a great sinner—fair in body but dark at heart, poison-faced rather than milk-faced. Naturally crooked, he does not resemble you; the wretch fails to recognize me as Death.’ Laughing, Lakṣmaṇa says, ‘Hear, sage: anger is the root of sin; under its control people act wrongly and move against the whole world.’",
