      "sequence": 290,
      "meaning": "Lakṣmaṇa continues: ‘I am your attendant, king of sages; abandon anger and show mercy now. The broken bow will not mend through rage. Please sit, since your feet must be aching. If it is very dear, arrange a remedy: summon some highly skilled person and have it joined.’ Janaka grows afraid as Lakṣmaṇa speaks and tells him to stop, for the impropriety is not good. The city’s men and women tremble, thinking the younger prince gravely at fault. Hearing fearless speech again and again, Bhṛgupati burns in anger and loses strength. He appeals to Rāma: ‘I spare him because I consider him your little brother. How can a beautiful body contain so sullied a mind? He is like a golden vessel filled with poison.’ Lakṣmaṇa smiles once more; Rāma glares at him, and he withdraws bashfully toward the guru, abandoning contrary speech.",
