    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0279",
      "sequence": 291,
      "meaning": "Rāma joins both hands and speaks in an exceedingly humble, gentle and cooling voice: ‘Hear me, lord, you are wise by nature. Pay no heed to a child’s words. Babbling belongs to childhood; no saint blames children for it. He has not spoiled anything; I am your offender. Grace, anger, killing or binding—master, deal with me as with your servant. Tell me quickly how your anger may depart, and I will do exactly that.’ The sage replies, ‘Rāma, how can the anger go while your younger brother still looks at me maliciously? If my axe has not reached his neck, what purpose has my anger served? At the terrible sweep and sound of this axe, earthly kings’ pregnant wives miscarry; yet with the axe still in my hand I see my enemy, this royal youth, alive.’",
