    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0280",
      "sequence": 292,
      "meaning": "Paraśurāma continues: ‘My hand will not act though anger burns my breast. Has the king-killing axe grown blunt? Fate has turned against me and my nature has changed—how has compassion arisen in my heart? Today this mercy makes me endure unbearable pain.’ Hearing him, the son of Sumitrā laughs and bows his head: ‘Ah, your favouring form is compassion itself; as you speak, words fall like flowers. If compassion burns your body, sage, then in anger only the Creator could preserve it! Look, Janaka—the stubborn boy wishes to build himself a home in Yama’s city. Why does he not quickly hide himself from my eyes? The little royal son is vicious to look upon.’ Lakṣmaṇa laughs inwardly: if one closes one’s eyes, no one exists anywhere. Paraśurāma then turns toward Rāma in deep fury: ‘Fool, after breaking Śambhu’s bow, you dare instruct me?’",
